WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.610
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:09.500
You know that I see a piece of fluid and I
can’t resist making videos about it…I

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.500
just can’t.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.889
Oh my goodness.

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:13.619
Look at that.

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:18.840
These animations were created using the Material
Point Method, or MPM in short, which is a

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.350
hybrid simulation method which is able to
simulate not only substances like water and

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:30.960
honey, but it can also simulate snow, granular
solids, cloth, and many-many other amazing

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.550
things that you see here.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.649
Before you ask, the hybrid part means that
it both uses particles and grids during the

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:39.649
computations.

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:44.249
Unfortunately, it is very computationally
demanding, so it takes forever to get these

00:00:44.249 --> 00:00:45.570
simulations ready.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.269
And typically, in my simulations, after this
step is done, I almost always find that the

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:55.719
objects did not line up perfectly, so I can
start the whole process again.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:57.089
Ah well.

00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:02.329
This technique has multiple stages, uses multiple
data structures in many of them, and often

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:07.660
we have to wait for the results of one stage
to be able to proceed to the next.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.220
This is not that much of a problem if we seek
to implement this on our processor, but it

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.940
would be way, way faster if we could run it
on the graphics card…as long as we implement

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.830
these problems on them properly.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:25.210
However, due to these stages waiting for each
other, it is immensely difficult to use the

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.220
heavy parallel computing capabilities of the
graphics card.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.680
So here you go, this technique enables running
MPM on your graphics card efficiently, resulting

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.580
in an up to ten-time improvement over previous
works.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:45.710
As a result, this granulation scene has more
than 6.5 million particles on a very fine

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:59.460
grid and can be simulated in only around 40
seconds per frame.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.860
And not only that, but the numerical stability
of this technique is also superior to previous

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.450
works, and it is thereby able to correctly
simulate how the individual grains interact

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.640
in this block of sand.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.550
Here is a more detailed breakdown of the number
of particles, grid resolutions, and the amount

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.690
of computation time needed to simulate each
time step.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:27.390
I am currently in the middle of a monstrous
fluid simulation project and oh man, I wish

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.470
I had these numbers for the computation time.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.500
This gelatin scene takes less than 7 seconds
per frame to simulate with a similar number

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.920
of particles.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.420
Look at that heavenly gooey thing.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.670
It probably tastes like strawberries.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.840
